WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.899
Now it's time to explore ways you can integrate pre-existing agents

00:00:02.899 --> 00:00:06.599
you may have built with any number of frameworks as NAT tools.

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:09.000
Compose multiple agents together

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:11.800
and see how NAT orchestrates different frameworks

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:13.800
to work seamlessly as a coordinated team.

00:00:14.208 --> 00:00:16.408
Let's get coding. In this lesson,

00:00:16.408 --> 00:00:19.859
we're going to take an existing powerful agent, a LangGraph agent,

00:00:20.109 --> 00:00:21.859
and we're going to expose it to

00:00:21.859 --> 00:00:23.609
our existing agent as an NAT tool.

00:00:24.158 --> 00:00:25.982
And this will show us how

00:00:25.982 --> 00:00:28.109
to extend workflow capabilities through agentic composition.

00:00:28.510 --> 00:00:32.320
We'll also see how to lift hard-coded providers such as LLMs

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:34.579
up out of our agent into configuration,

00:00:34.579 --> 00:00:37.740
which makes it easier to experiment and iterate later on.

00:00:38.549 --> 00:00:41.609
We'll see how NAT orchestrates multiple agent frameworks together,

00:00:41.909 --> 00:00:43.202
all of this means that we'll

00:00:43.202 --> 00:00:44.710
be able to build more powerful workflows

00:00:44.710 --> 00:00:46.670
by combining existing agents.

00:00:47.048 --> 00:00:49.048
Sometimes tools just aren't enough.

00:00:49.347 --> 00:00:51.347
For instance, our current Climate Analyzer.

00:00:51.347 --> 00:00:53.548
It can retrieve temperature data.

00:00:53.548 --> 00:00:56.347
It can calculate basic statistics and it can create visualizations.

00:00:56.847 --> 00:00:59.847
But more complicated math, such as compound growth rates

00:00:59.847 --> 00:01:02.847
or multi-step calculations or complex projections,

00:01:02.847 --> 00:01:04.601
it can't do these things. But

00:01:04.601 --> 00:01:06.647
what if we had an existing agent?

00:01:06.647 --> 00:01:09.548
In fact, we do, this LangGraph calculator agent.

00:01:10.093 --> 00:01:13.493
It can handle complex calculations as we'll see in the notebook.

00:01:13.894 --> 00:01:16.394
So how do we integrate that LangGraph agent

00:01:16.394 --> 00:01:18.694
into our existing agentic workflow?

00:01:19.093 --> 00:01:20.694
We just register it as another function.

00:01:21.394 --> 00:01:24.093
So we've already seen this register_function decorator.

00:01:24.236 --> 00:01:27.037
We've already seen this boilerplate that we use to wrap a function.

00:01:27.537 --> 00:01:30.736
We've already seen most of this and it's all it takes

00:01:30.736 --> 00:01:32.887
to wrap an existing complex agent and

00:01:32.887 --> 00:01:35.037
make it available to our agentic workflow.

00:01:35.313 --> 00:01:37.160
Let's look at our configuration again

00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:39.313
now that we've integrated this separate agent.

00:01:40.013 --> 00:01:42.813
We have a new function, a calculator agent.

00:01:43.213 --> 00:01:45.164
And this calculator agent wraps the entire

00:01:45.164 --> 00:01:47.114
LangGraph agent that we looked at before.

00:01:47.813 --> 00:01:50.114
And if you look under tool_names and workflow,

00:01:50.114 --> 00:01:52.813
you'll see that we've exposed the calculator_agent as just another tool.

00:01:53.114 --> 00:01:57.114
We've also added another LLM. We have a climate_llm from before,

00:01:57.114 --> 00:01:58.808
but now we have a calculator_llm.

00:01:58.808 --> 00:02:02.608
This isn't required, we could use the same LLM for multiple tools,

00:02:02.608 --> 00:02:05.108
but in our case we wanted a new LLM definition

00:02:05.108 --> 00:02:07.209
so we can constrain the max_tokens.

00:02:07.209 --> 00:02:09.138
You might have other agents that have other

00:02:09.138 --> 00:02:11.308
properties that need to be changed such as temperature.

00:02:11.467 --> 00:02:15.266
And now we can begin to understand the power of agentic composition.

00:02:15.967 --> 00:02:18.266
In this picture, we see a workflow on the left

00:02:18.467 --> 00:02:20.467
and it has access to three tools.

00:02:20.967 --> 00:02:24.266
But the top tool and the bottom tool also have tools.

00:02:24.866 --> 00:02:27.467
These tools are actually agents themselves.

00:02:27.944 --> 00:02:31.245
and some of the tools that those agents have also contain tools.

00:02:31.245 --> 00:02:33.544
Those tools are also full agents.

00:02:33.944 --> 00:02:36.250
And instead of having a bunch of different

00:02:36.250 --> 00:02:38.844
agents running separately, now we can compose them together.

00:02:38.944 --> 00:02:42.444
Agents communicating with agents and leveraging each other's powers.

00:02:42.788 --> 00:02:46.288
What this means is we can extend an agent with another agent.

00:02:46.788 --> 00:02:48.588
Before we did the integration, for instance,

00:02:48.989 --> 00:02:50.650
we couldn't ask for mathematical calculations

00:02:50.650 --> 00:02:52.588
to be done against our climate data.

00:02:52.989 --> 00:02:55.112
But after integration, now we can

00:02:55.112 --> 00:02:57.588
combine the power of our calculator agent

00:02:57.588 --> 00:02:59.588
with the power of our climate agent. And we

00:02:59.588 --> 00:03:01.588
can get much better answers and much better analysis.

00:03:02.064 --> 00:03:04.664
And this is the power of unified orchestration.

00:03:05.064 --> 00:03:07.865
Do you have a LangGraph agent, a CrewAI agent,

00:03:08.264 --> 00:03:10.365
some custom FastAPI service?

00:03:10.865 --> 00:03:13.264
You can wrap all of these and orchestrate them

00:03:13.264 --> 00:03:15.664
by NAT's unified orchestration layer.

00:03:16.064 --> 00:03:18.865
In this lesson, we are going to be looking at

00:03:18.865 --> 00:03:20.764
integrating multiple agents together.

00:03:20.772 --> 00:03:23.372
First, let's install our climate analyzer package

00:03:23.372 --> 00:03:25.272
that we looked at in previous notebooks.

00:03:25.772 --> 00:03:27.372
All right, and now that that's installed,

00:03:27.573 --> 00:03:29.573
let's think about some limitations that our

00:03:29.573 --> 00:03:32.372
current climate analyzer agent cannot handle.

00:03:33.073 --> 00:03:35.772
So let's look at some questions that our agent can't handle yet.

00:03:36.051 --> 00:03:38.123
If we want to observe temperature data

00:03:38.123 --> 00:03:40.491
but look at the compound annual growth rate,

00:03:40.951 --> 00:03:42.532
it's not going to be able to calculate that

00:03:42.532 --> 00:03:45.592
because it's a climate agent, not a mathematical agent.

00:03:46.181 --> 00:03:48.622
If we wanted to get the weighted average of population,

00:03:48.981 --> 00:03:50.921
again, this is a mathematical question and our

00:03:50.921 --> 00:03:52.861
climate agent is going to stumble on it.

00:03:53.651 --> 00:03:55.913
And if we want to ask

00:03:55.913 --> 00:03:58.551
a question about renewable capacity, projected growth,

00:03:58.551 --> 00:04:01.252
when are we going to hit a certain amount of gigawatts?

00:04:01.602 --> 00:04:03.581
Our agent's not going to be able to handle that either.

00:04:03.917 --> 00:04:05.440
It's a great climate agent, but

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:07.217
it doesn't know how to do math.

00:04:08.007 --> 00:04:10.806
Now we have another agent here. It's a completely separate agent.

00:04:11.206 --> 00:04:14.306
It's not at all associated with the NeMo Agent Toolkit.

00:04:14.706 --> 00:04:16.906
It is a LangGraph agent and it's a calculator.

00:04:17.406 --> 00:04:19.500
It's already built, it's battle-tested, it's ready to

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:21.593
use, and it knows how to do math.

00:04:21.593 --> 00:04:24.192
Let's take a look at the calculator agent structure.

00:04:24.593 --> 00:04:28.492
So this is a multi-step calculator agent and it's written in LangGraph.

00:04:28.793 --> 00:04:31.992
And it has a number of mathematical tools available to it.

00:04:32.093 --> 00:04:33.976
For instance, basic math,

00:04:33.976 --> 00:04:36.076
calculating a percentage_change,

00:04:37.177 --> 00:04:39.177
calculating the compound_growth_rate,

00:04:39.476 --> 00:04:41.476
and other tools that are available.

00:04:41.976 --> 00:04:45.476
Let's set up a difficult question for this mathematical agent to calculate.

00:04:45.973 --> 00:04:50.173
We're going to go ahead and put climate data into the prompt.

00:04:50.773 --> 00:04:53.773
So we're going to put in a country's emissions.

00:04:54.370 --> 00:04:56.370
and how those emissions reduced.

00:04:57.271 --> 00:04:58.821
And then we're going to ask it

00:04:58.821 --> 00:05:00.370
to do some calculations on those emissions.

00:05:01.670 --> 00:05:03.471
And now the agent has run with that

00:05:03.471 --> 00:05:05.271
prompt and it's given us a final result.

00:05:05.571 --> 00:05:07.221
We can see its reasoning as it's

00:05:07.221 --> 00:05:08.870
calculating the emissions in the various years.

00:05:09.071 --> 00:05:12.571
And finally, it gives us calculated emissions for the year 2025.

00:05:13.065 --> 00:05:14.966
So we can see that it's able

00:05:14.966 --> 00:05:16.865
to perform a variety of mathematical functions.

00:05:17.865 --> 00:05:19.865
Let's switch back to our climate agent.

00:05:20.065 --> 00:05:24.165
We give our climate agent a question on temperature statistics in India

00:05:24.365 --> 00:05:25.865
and while it's going to be able

00:05:25.865 --> 00:05:27.365
to get the climate information for that,

00:05:27.466 --> 00:05:29.216
it's not going to be able to do the

00:05:29.216 --> 00:05:30.966
math. Let's run this workflow and watch it fail.

00:05:31.404 --> 00:05:33.985
So we can see the agent trying to solve this problem.

00:05:34.504 --> 00:05:36.404
It begins to calculate statistics for India,

00:05:36.404 --> 00:05:39.884
but we can see that it calls this function multiple times,

00:05:40.105 --> 00:05:42.504
trying to calculate statistics over various years,

00:05:42.504 --> 00:05:44.805
including years that are not on our data set.

00:05:45.204 --> 00:05:48.504
It thrashes for a while and then finally hallucinates something

00:05:48.704 --> 00:05:50.704
in an effort to give us the answer that we want.

00:05:51.230 --> 00:05:53.329
So what happened?

00:05:53.329 --> 00:05:56.129
It retrieved the temperature data correctly as we would expect it to,

00:05:56.230 --> 00:05:58.230
and it knows the values that it

00:05:58.230 --> 00:06:00.230
needs, but it can't do the math.

00:06:00.329 --> 00:06:03.029
And so it's most likely going to hallucinate answers.

00:06:03.329 --> 00:06:04.870
What we need to do is integrate

00:06:04.870 --> 00:06:06.629
the calculator agent that we looked at earlier.

00:06:06.827 --> 00:06:09.177
So how do we integrate that LangGraph

00:06:09.177 --> 00:06:11.528
Agent into our NeMo Agent Toolkit workflow?

00:06:12.028 --> 00:06:14.927
We've already seen how to integrate a simple Python function

00:06:14.927 --> 00:06:18.327
and this is no different. It's just another function.

00:06:18.779 --> 00:06:21.079
We've already seen this boilerplate code before

00:06:21.079 --> 00:06:23.680
how to take a Python function and wrap it

00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:25.572
so that it's exposed as a

00:06:25.572 --> 00:06:27.779
tool. There are just a few changes.

00:06:28.279 --> 00:06:31.279
First, when we decorate our function with this register_function,

00:06:32.279 --> 00:06:35.279
we can now say that the framework wrapper is LangChain.

00:06:35.648 --> 00:06:39.747
because our agent is a LangGraph and therefore LangChain agent.

00:06:40.247 --> 00:06:43.648
This allows the NeMo Agent Toolkit to, for instance,

00:06:43.648 --> 00:06:48.247
better integrate observability and evaluation tools into this wrapped agent.

00:06:48.848 --> 00:06:51.447
Something else that we do, we've seen the builder

00:06:51.648 --> 00:06:54.447
that was injected into the wrapping function before,

00:06:54.548 --> 00:06:57.247
but we didn't use this builder. But now we can.

00:06:57.447 --> 00:06:59.447
We can ask the builder for an LLM.

00:06:59.812 --> 00:07:01.911
And that's what we do here.

00:07:02.012 --> 00:07:05.612
Instead of hardcoding the LLM into our calculator agent,

00:07:05.812 --> 00:07:08.026
now we can control that LLM

00:07:08.026 --> 00:07:10.608
through the NeMo Agent Toolkit YAML file.

00:07:10.608 --> 00:07:13.908
So we request a specific LLM called calculator LLM

00:07:14.408 --> 00:07:16.808
and we tell it that it needs to wrap that

00:07:16.808 --> 00:07:19.209
LLM in an adapter that works with a LangChain agent.

00:07:19.509 --> 00:07:22.809
And then we're able to create our calculator agent with that LLM.

00:07:23.408 --> 00:07:25.088
All of the rest of this will

00:07:25.088 --> 00:07:27.009
be familiar to you. We have our wrapper

00:07:27.021 --> 00:07:30.920
function. Inside that wrapper function, we call the agent itself

00:07:30.920 --> 00:07:33.090
and we return the results. And

00:07:33.090 --> 00:07:35.620
then finally, we yield a FunctionInfo object

00:07:36.120 --> 00:07:38.420
that tells our NeMo Agent Toolkit

00:07:38.821 --> 00:07:40.920
workflow how to talk to this agent.

00:07:41.290 --> 00:07:43.124
Now that we've prepared our calculator agent

00:07:43.124 --> 00:07:45.220
to be used by the NeMo Agent Toolkit,

00:07:45.220 --> 00:07:47.779
let's look at what the configuration file will look like.

00:07:48.019 --> 00:07:51.579
We've already seen in our LLM section this climate LLM,

00:07:51.579 --> 00:07:54.399
and while we could reuse this for the calculator agent,

00:07:54.399 --> 00:07:56.800
let's give it its own dedicated LLM.

00:07:57.235 --> 00:07:59.435
We'll call this calculator_llm.

00:07:59.735 --> 00:08:02.535
We saw this earlier when we asked the builder for a calculator_llm.

00:08:02.535 --> 00:08:04.242
This is how it finds it.

00:08:04.242 --> 00:08:06.235
And this will also be a NIM.

00:08:06.735 --> 00:08:08.735
We'll use similar parameters to before,

00:08:08.735 --> 00:08:11.735
except let's change the max_tokens to 1024.

00:08:12.259 --> 00:08:16.689
because we should be outputting less tokens to do mathematical calculations.

00:08:17.259 --> 00:08:20.158
Our climate tools remain unchanged and they're elided here.

00:08:21.059 --> 00:08:24.658
But we will register the calculator_agent simply as a function.

00:08:25.459 --> 00:08:29.358
This is the entire calculator_agent now exposed as a simple tool.

00:08:29.630 --> 00:08:31.850
and we can take this tool and

00:08:31.850 --> 00:08:34.071
plug it into our ReAct agent workflow.

00:08:34.671 --> 00:08:37.370
Now our ReAct agent is going to have available to it

00:08:37.581 --> 00:08:40.811
all of the climate functions and the calculator agent.

00:08:41.630 --> 00:08:44.130
So what have we done to take this LangGraph agent

00:08:44.211 --> 00:08:46.390
and make it available to the NeMo Agent Toolkit?

00:08:46.787 --> 00:08:48.986
First, we've removed the LLM

00:08:48.986 --> 00:08:51.586
that was hardcoded in the LangGraph agent

00:08:51.586 --> 00:08:54.586
and we've moved it into the NAT config.

00:08:54.586 --> 00:08:56.986
That way it's not hardcoded and it's configurable.

00:08:57.375 --> 00:09:01.365
Second, we've told NeMo Agent Toolkit that it is a LangChain agent.

00:09:01.875 --> 00:09:06.004
This gives greater compatibility between NAT and the wrapped agent.

00:09:07.004 --> 00:09:09.605
As for the registration, it's just another tool to NAT.

00:09:09.705 --> 00:09:12.304
It's another Python function that is now available

00:09:12.404 --> 00:09:14.605
for any NAT agent workflow to call.

00:09:15.206 --> 00:09:16.776
Let's run the same question that

00:09:16.776 --> 00:09:18.606
we did before with the new config

00:09:18.606 --> 00:09:20.907
and see if it's able to use the mathematical agent.

00:09:22.006 --> 00:09:24.407
We can see it getting climate information from India.

00:09:24.907 --> 00:09:27.307
And now when it goes to calculate the temperature statistics,

00:09:27.307 --> 00:09:30.006
we can see that it's decided to call our calculator_agent.

00:09:30.307 --> 00:09:32.907
The calculator_agent runs and we can see its output there.

00:09:34.006 --> 00:09:38.006
And finally, it arrives at a non-hallucinated answer

00:09:38.006 --> 00:09:40.106
that the temperature in India in 2050

00:09:40.106 --> 00:09:42.907
will be approximately 25.24 degrees.

00:09:43.360 --> 00:09:46.159
And finally, let's uninstall our climate analyzer package

00:09:46.159 --> 00:09:48.259
just so we're ready to run the next notebook.

00:09:48.559 --> 00:09:50.559
So what have we done in this notebook?

00:09:50.559 --> 00:09:53.860
We added mathematical capabilities to our climate agent

00:09:54.159 --> 00:09:56.432
by pulling in another existing agent.

00:09:56.432 --> 00:09:59.133
and we saw that to the NeMo Agent Toolkit,

00:09:59.232 --> 00:10:01.232
it's just another Python function.

00:10:01.932 --> 00:10:04.133
We also saw that we can deeply integrate

00:10:04.232 --> 00:10:06.332
NAT into our existing agentic workflows

00:10:06.932 --> 00:10:10.232
to allow its config-driven development to work through all of our agents.

00:10:10.633 --> 00:10:13.033
And now our climate agent can handle complex math.
